  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Fixes\n\nType parameters named await, file, required, scoped or extension (#378, closes #369). A target or method type parameter with one of these names made the imposter fail to compile:\n\nfile and required from C# 11, read as modifiers;\n\nextension from C# 14, read as an extension block;\n\nscoped from C# 11;\n\nawait in async methods.\n\nA type parameter named like any contextual keyword your compiler knows now takes another name in generated code, as var, \\_, record and partial already did. That covers keywords later compilers add, such as the C# 15 preview's closed and union. You still write the imposter and its setups with your own type arguments, and dedicated namespaces keep the original names.\n\nMethod type parameters named like members of their generated types (#373, closes #349). A generic method whose type parameter was named Invoke, Returns, Matches, As, Callback, Then, Result or another member of the types generated for it failed with CS0102. Such a type parameter now takes another name in generated code; your setups don't change.\n\nMaintenance\n\nRegression tests for inherited generic methods whose type parameter is named like the target's (#368, closes #350); the fix itself shipped in 0.2.23 with #362. The generator also lost an unused helper (#375, closes #372). The generated imposters behave the same.",
